 # Living Survey: brain                                                                                        
-                                                                                                              
-## Introduction                                                                                               
-                                                                                                              
-This document collects the scientific literature regarding **brain**.                                         
-                                                                                                              
-## Recent Advances in Brain Research                                                                          
-                                                                                                              
-### Brain Imaging, Segmentation, and Clinical Applications                                                    
-Recent developments in neuroimaging and medical image analysis have significantly improved the precision of   
brain tumor segmentation and disease progression modeling. Deep learning frameworks like **NeuroTS-Net** and   
**De-GAN** have advanced multi-class semantic segmentation of pediatric brain tumors and contrast-enhancing    
FLAIR synthesis, respectively, outperforming baseline methods in Dice scores and handling domain shifts        
[^2609.16873][^2609.16755]. Foundation models such as **BrainVLM** integrate multi-modal data to classify WHO  
brain tumor types with diagnostic uncertainty quantification [^2609.16597], while **GRIN+** addresses machine  
unlearning for imbalanced medical data to protect patient privacy without compromising rare condition diagnosis
[^2609.15571]. Furthermore, **nnU-Net** generalization across heterogeneous brain tumor populations has been   
rigorously evaluated, highlighting challenges in small tumor volumes [^2609.15524]. In the realm of diffusion  
MRI, **DTI-SHNet** enables single-to-multi-shell synthesis, preserving downstream diffusion measures           
[^2609.14312]. Clinical assessment tools have also evolved, with **TractSpLearn** detecting subtle white matter
alterations in traumatic brain injury [^2609.15342] and reverse spatio-temporal modeling reconstructing        
healthier brain states from Alzheimer's disease progression [^2609.14590]. Ex-vivo calf brain models with      
integrated force sensors now allow high-fidelity quantification of tool-tissue interactions during simulated   
neurosurgery [^2609.14198], and 4D cerebral microvascular imaging platforms visualize hemodynamic responses in 
stroke models [^2609.16666].                                                                                   
-                                                                                                              
-### EEG, Brain Signal Analysis, and Digital Twins                                                             
-The analysis of electroencephalography (EEG) and brain signals has seen a surge in foundation models and      
zero-shot learning capabilities. **METIS** establishes a new paradigm for multi-task brain signal analysis,    
pretrained on over 70,000 hours of recordings and outperforming generalist models in zero-shot evaluations     
[^2609.15740]. Similarly, **iMINDBench** provides a standardized benchmark for iEEG neural decoding across     
institutions, revealing the limits of scaling supervised data [^2609.18104]. Generative decoding has also      
advanced, with **Mind2Cloud** utilizing two-granularity diffusion decoding to reconstruct 3D point clouds from 
EEG signals [^2609.13991]. Transformer-based frameworks with spectrogram representations have improved         
schizophrenia detection from EEG [^2609.14015], while studies on infant EEG microstates link temperament       
attributes to specific brain topographies [^10.7273_000007929]. On the modeling front, **brain-based digital   
twins** (BB-DT) fuse EEG with kinematic data for real-time motor imagery feedback [^10.17870_bathspa.00017805],
and stochastic models combining Random Neural Networks with Markov-Modulated Poisson Processes aim to improve  
the fidelity of brain digital twins [^2609.13973].                                                             
-                                                                                                              
-### Computational Neuroscience and Brain Connectivity                                                         
-Theoretical and computational approaches continue to unravel brain dynamics and structural fingerprints.      
**Graphlets** serve as structural fingerprints for complex networks, capturing local-to-mesoscale topology in  
resting-state functional connectomes and schizophrenia-related alterations [^2609.17445]. Nonparametric        
correlation estimators solving cubic equations have been applied to analyze dynamic functional connectivity    
across six frequency bands [^2609.13848]. Theoretical models suggest that moderate neural noise, modeled as    
temporally correlated fluctuations, is critical for faithful internal simulation of rare events in Bayesian    
Confidence Propagation Neural Networks (BCPNN) [^2609.18033]. Evolutionary neuro-computational studies         
challenge the Cognitive Buffer Hypothesis, showing that larger neural networks in dynamic environments often   
arise from prior static evolution [^2609.15569]. Additionally, spatial codes governing olfactory receptor      
choice align sensory maps in the nose and brain, revealing a continuously varying transcriptional code for     
smell [^10.5281_zenodo.18941307][^10.5281_zenodo.18941308], while cholecystokinin signaling in the nucleus of  
the solitary tract modulates satiety and is blunted by high-fat diets [^10.7273_000008411].                    
-                                                                                                              
-### Neuro-Oncology, Disease Mechanisms, and Metabolism                                                        
-Research into brain diseases and metabolic profiling continues to expand. Glioblastoma (GBM) remains a focal  
point, with studies on nucleosome repositioning in cfDNA offering non-invasive liquid biopsy diagnostics       
[^10.5526_err-00042862][^10.5526_err-00042900]. Lipid-based nanoparticles and LNP-encapsulated siRNA targeting 
TERT show promise in overcoming the blood-brain barrier for GBM therapy                                        
[^10.17030_uclan.thesis.00059253][^10.17030_uclan.thesis.00059033], while secretomes from neural crest-derived 
stem cells inhibit GBM proliferation and modulate immune responses [^10.48683_1926.00128572]. Pan-cancer brain 
metastasis atlases provide spatially resolved single-cell data                                                 
[^10.5281_zenodo.21816284][^10.5281_zenodo.21816285][^10.5281_zenodo.21782885][^10.5281_zenodo.21782884], and  
RET signaling is identified as a mediator in estrogen receptor-positive breast cancer brain metastasis         
[^10.5281_zenodo.19501083][^10.5281_zenodo.19501082]. Beyond oncology, comprehensive arteriovenous metabolic   
profiling reveals cross-organ metabolite exchange, highlighting brain-specific uptake and release patterns     
[^10.5281_zenodo.20652506][^10.5281_zenodo.20652505]. In neurodegenerative contexts, the amyloid precursor     
protein (APP) is re-evaluated as a universal survival molecule, critiquing broad-spectrum APP suppression      
therapies for Alzheimer's [^10.5281_zenodo.20049820], and nitric oxide signaling is linked to aerobic          
exercise-related neuroprotection in Parkinson's disease via mitophagy modulation                               
[^10.4103_mgr.medgasres-d-26-00008]. Finally, circular RNAs (circRNAs) accumulate in the brain corresponding to
biological age, serving as potential biomarkers for aging and insulin-related pathways [^10.48617_etd.1568],   
while category fluency tasks reveal cultural and age-related gray matter volume associations in semantic       
processing [^10.48617_etd.1671].                                                                               
